                  "value": "Zhang, Xiaolu"
                },
                "datasetContactAffiliation": {
                  "typeName": "datasetContactAffiliation",
                  "multiple": false,
                  "typeClass": "primitive",
                  "value": "Tilburg University, Tilburg School of Social and Behavioral Sciences, Department of Social Psychology"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "typeName": "dsDescription",
            "multiple": true,
            "typeClass": "compound",
            "value": [
              {
                "dsDescriptionValue": {
                  "typeName": "dsDescriptionValue",
                  "multiple": false,
                  "typeClass": "primitive",
                  "value": "To lend money to someone and to later ask this same person to\npay the money back should be relatively unproblematic in modern,\nmonetized societies. Still, some people find it difficult to ask for\nlent money to be paid back, even though it is in their own interest\nthat this happens and they have the legitimate right to ask their\nmoney back. In this article, we examine one reason why people\nmight experience such difficulties: the anticipation of guilt. In Study\n4.1, the majority of participants from 3 different countries indicated\nthat they sometimes did not ask money back because doing so\nwould make them feel guilty. Study 4.2 found that the more people\nanticipated guilt about asking their money back, the less willing\nthey were to do this. Study 4.3 found that the effect of guilt\nbecame less strong when more money was at stake. Study 4.4\nfound that people anticipated more guilt and were less likely to ask\nmoney back when the other person was poor compared to rich.\nStudies 4.5 and 4.6 found that the amount of harm people\nanticipated by asking the money back mediated the effect. Taken\ntogether, we interpret these studies (Ntotal = 2988) to showcase\nthe social nature of guilt, in that it can motivate people to sacrifice\ntheir (financial) self-interest in order to protect relationships with\nothers."
                }
              }
            ]
          },
